# Entity Schemas

Spec: EntitySchemas
Purpose:
  Define schemas for knowledge entities introduced in Phase 139 that
  are not yet covered by existing entity definitions. These entities
  populate the knowledge lifecycle pipeline and the claim/challenge
  system, enabling auditable knowledge evolution with confidence
  tracking, version lineage, and drift detection.

Owns: lifecycle/entity-schemas.sdd

## Entity Types

### @observation.*
  Purpose: Raw AI observations — the L0 entry point of the learning loop.
  Fields:
    id:         @observation.<name>
    observed_in: <@tmp.* or @project.* or @task.*>
    type:       pattern | anti_pattern | smell | gap | drift | terminology_drift
    description: one-sentence description of what was observed
    detail:     multi-line explanation with context
    evidence:   [<@code.* | @test.* | @task.* | @project.*>]
    timestamp:  YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ
    confidence: low (always low at L0)
    trust:      low (always low at L0)
    status:     observed
    promoted_to: optional @discovery.* or @candidate.*

  Example:
    @observation.O88
      type: pattern
      observed_in: @project.payment
      description: DB transaction + event publishing frequently cause inconsistency
      evidence:
        - @code.payment.transaction.go
      timestamp: 2026-09-01T10:30:00Z
      confidence: low
      trust: low
      status: observed
      promoted_to: @discovery.D44

### @discovery.*
  Purpose: L1 discovery — a repeated or strategically important pattern
  observed across multiple contexts.
  Fields:
    id:          @discovery.<name>
    observations: [@observation.*] (source observations)
    description:   one-sentence summary of the discovery
    detail:       multi-line explanation
    first_seen:    YYYY-MM-DD
    occurrences:   integer (number of independent contexts)
    domains:       [<domain names where observed>]
    confidence:    low | medium
    trust:          low (default — not yet validated)
    status:         discovered
    promoted_to:   optional @candidate.*

  Example:
    @discovery.D44
      observations:
        - @observation.O88
        - @observation.O89
        - @observation.O90
      description: Transaction-event inconsistency is a widespread pattern
      occurrences: 3
      domains: [payment, order, inventory]
      confidence: low
      trust: low
      status: discovered
      promoted_to: @candidate.transaction-event-consistency

### @candidate.*
  Purpose: L2 candidate — reusable knowledge that may become a validated
  skill or concept. Carries the Phase 139 candidate lifecycle status.
  Fields:
    id:            @candidate.<name>
    discovery:      @discovery.* (originating discovery)
    description:    one-sentence summary
    detail:         multi-line definition
    aliases:        [alternative names]
    related:        [@concept.* | @candidate.*]
    evidence:       [<@observation.* | @code.* | @test.* | @task.*>]
    evidence_count: integer
    confidence:     low | medium
    confidence_score: float (0.0–1.0)
    trust:            low (default)
    status:            candidate
    lifecycle_status: CANDIDATE | ACTIVE | STALE | DEPRECATED | ARCHIVED
    conflicts_with:   [@reference.* | @skill.*]
    promotion_target: optional @skill.* / @concept.* on promotion
    promoted_to:      @skill.* / @concept.* (on success)
    expired_at:        YYYY-MM-DD (30 days from creation)
    generation:         integer (starts at 1)

### @claim.*
  Purpose: A claim represents a single, assertable truth about the
  system — a statement that the project adopts, believes, or enforces.
  Claims are the claim layer between evidence and knowledge.
  Fields:
    id:             @claim.<name>
    statement:       single-sentence assertion
    confidence:       LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH
    confidence_score: float (0.0–1.0)
    supported_by:     [@evidence.* | @code.* | @test.* | @decision.* | @observation.*]
    challenged_by:    [@challenge.*]
    status:            PROPOSED | SUPPORTED | VALIDATED | ACTIVE | CHALLENGED | INVALIDATED | SUPERSEDED
    domain:            <project domain name>
    scope:             local | project | global
    origin:            @discovery.* | @observation.* | @insight.*
    provenance:
      discovered_from: <@reference or @tmp>
      source: <external source / repository / project>
      extracted_by: AI | human | @human
      validated_by: <@task.* | @human>
      validated_at: YYYY-MM-DD
    supersedes:      optional @claim.* (if this claim replaces another)
    superseded_by:   optional @claim.* (if this claim was replaced)
    generation:      integer

  Example:
    @claim.C11
      statement: Payment retries must be idempotent
      confidence: HIGH
      confidence_score: 0.91
      supported_by:
        - @evidence.E31
        - @decision.D88
      status: VALIDATED
      domain: payment
      scope: global
      origin: @discovery.D44
      provenance:
        discovered_from: @tmp.payment-service
        source: payment-service repository
        extracted_by: AI
        validated_by: @task.1044
        validated_at: 2026-08-31
      generation: 1

### @challenge.*
  Purpose: A challenge contests a claim with counter-evidence.
  Fields:
    id:                @challenge.<name>
    claim:             @claim.* (the claim being challenged)
    counter_evidence:  [@evidence.* | @code.* | @test.* | @observation.*]
    reason:             why the claim may be wrong
    proposed_resolution: INVALIDATE | MODIFY | SCOPE_LIMIT | REJECT_CHALLENGE
    status:             CHALLENGED | RESOLVED
    reviewed_by:        @task.* | @human
    reviewed_at:         YYYY-MM-DD
    outcome:             ACCEPTED | REJECTED
    evidence_count:      integer

  Example:
    @challenge.C44
      claim: @claim.C11
      counter_evidence:
        - @evidence.E91
      reason: New provider behavior contradicts idempotency assumption
      proposed_resolution: MODIFY
      status: RESOLVED
      reviewed_by: @human
      reviewed_at: 2026-09-01
      outcome: ACCEPTED
      evidence_count: 1

### @generation.*
  Purpose: Track version lineage of a skill, concept, or claim.
  Fields:
    id:          @generation.<name>
    entity:      @skill.* | @concept.* | @claim.*
    generations:
      - { gen: 1, at: YYYY-MM-DD, by: @discovery.*, reason: "<initial>" }
      - { gen: 2, at: YYYY-MM-DD, by: @task.*, reason: "<refinement>" }
      - { gen: 3, at: YYYY-MM-DD, by: @challenge.*, reason: "<challenge>" }
    lineage_graph: [<entity_id>] (ordered list of ancestor entities)

### @violation.*
  Purpose: Architecture drift violation — detected mismatch between
  declared architecture and observed code.
  Fields:
    id:          @violation.<name>
    principle:    @architecture.* | @concept.* | project_policy
    found_in:     <file path or @code.*>
    reason:       description of the violation
    severity:     LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH | CRITICAL
    status:       DETECTED | REVIEWED | ACCEPTED | FALSE_POSITIVE | FIXED | VERIFIED
    detected_at:  YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ
    accepted_by:  optional @decision.* (if violation is knowingly accepted)
    accepted_reason: optional explanation
    exception:    optional @exception.* (if covered by an exception)

### @exception.*
  Purpose: Architecture exception — a deliberate, time-limited deviation
  from an architecture rule.
  Fields:
    id:          @exception.<name>
    rule:        @architecture.* | @concept.* | project_policy
    exception:    <entity or module granted the exception>
    reason:       why the exception is needed
    expires:      YYYY-MM-DD
    approved_by:  @human | @decision.*
    status:       ACTIVE | EXPIRED | REVOKED

  Example:
    @exception.E22
      rule: @architecture.ddd (domain cannot access infrastructure)
      exception: RefundProviderAdapter
      reason: legacy integration with external provider
      expires: 2026-12-01
      approved_by: @human
      status: ACTIVE

## State
  State: + (new entity types for Phase 139 knowledge lifecycle)

